export anchor DuplicateExports:
    description:
        There are four ways a name can leave a module. It can be a declaration
        marked export, `export Name`, `from ./X export Name`, or `from ./X
        export *`, which hands on everything X exports. The first three are
        explicit exports. The last is a glob.

        Two `export *` lines can export the same name with two different
        meanings. That is not an error. The module exports every one of them,
        and the compiler changes the output to keep them apart.

        ```piton fragment
        \\\
        from ./cruxible export *
        from ./strataglyph export *
        \\\
        ```

        If both modules export an anchor called Roles, the module does not
        export the plain name Roles, because it cannot mean one of them over the
        other. It exports each one under the name of its source module
        instead: cruxible.Roles and strataglyph.Roles. The name of a source
        module is its file name without .pi, or the name of its directory when
        it is an index.pi. A name that only one glob exports, or that all the
        globs export with the same meaning, stays a plain export.

        Two source modules can have the same name, for example when two packages
        each have a module called roles. Then the compiler adds the name of the
        parent directory to each of those qualifiers, and does this again until
        each qualifier is unique. The parent directory of an installed package
        module is the package name. Thus `from ./pkg-a/roles export *` and `from
        ./pkg-b/roles export *` export pkg-a.roles.Roles and pkg-b.roles.Roles.
        Only the qualifiers that collide get longer. As a last way to tell an
        index.pi apart from a file with the name of its directory, its qualifier
        gets index.

        The compiled output follows the same names. Here the module compiles to
        an object with the keys cruxible.Roles and strataglyph.Roles, next to
        the keys of its plain exports, in the order of the export lines.

        An import selects an overlapping export by its qualified name. Without
        an alias, the import binds the last part of the name.

        ```piton fragment
        \\\
        from ./index import cruxible.Roles, strataglyph.Roles GlyphRoles
        \\\
        ```

        This brings in Roles from cruxible and GlyphRoles from strataglyph. A
        longer qualifier works the same way: the import uses the full name, for
        example `from ./index import pkg-a.roles.Roles`. An import of the plain
        name Roles from that index is an error, because the index does not
        export it. The error names each qualified name that the import can use
        instead. A `from ./index export *` line in another module hands on the
        qualified names as they are.

        An explicit export hides the same name from a glob, and that is not an
        error either. If the module declares or names its own Roles, it exports
        that Roles, and the Roles of each glob stays out.

        Exporting the same thing twice under one name isn't a collision. Two
        `export *` lines that both end up at the same anchor (say, because both
        modules re-export it from a shared file) export one thing, and that's
        fine.

        Only one case is a compile error, `duplicate-export`: two explicit
        exports of one name with two different meanings. Overlapping globs are
        never this error, because their qualifiers are always unique. The error
        names the clashing name and both modules it comes from. To fix it,
        export one of them under an alias.

        ```piton fragment
        \\\
        from ./a export Roles
        from ./b export Roles BRoles
        \\\
        ```

        A duplicate export is still an error, but the compiler keeps one of the
        two so the rest of the program can resolve and every other problem gets
        reported in the same run. A declaration marked export wins first, then
        the other named exports in the order they are written, then the `export
        *` lines in the order they are written. The one that isn't kept isn't
        exported by the module, so if nothing else reaches it, `piton reach`
        reports it as unreachable. The language server reports the same error,
        since it shares the compiler's diagnostics.
